.TH RUSHX 1 "March 2026" "RushX 1.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rushx \- POSIX-style terminal emulator and shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rushx
[\fB\-\-rushx\-shell\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
RushX (Rust Shell \- eXtended) is a single-binary Linux terminal emulator and
shell implemented in Rust. By default it launches a GTK4-based terminal emulator
window that allocates a pseudoterminal (PTY), spawns itself as a child process
in shell mode, and renders output with full ANSI color support. With
\fB\-\-rushx\-shell\fR it runs the interactive shell directly on the calling
terminal.
.PP
The terminal emulator provides a bidirectional I/O pipeline between the user and
the shell: a reader thread, a 16 ms poll timer, an ANSI/VT100 escape sequence
parser with SGR color and attribute rendering (16-color Catppuccin Mocha palette,
256-color indexed, 24-bit RGB), and Cairo-based text drawing with per-segment
styling. A blink cursor and system bell (BEL) are supported.
.PP
The shell operates in raw terminal mode, providing a user@hostname:cwd prompt,
character-by-character input, and interactive tab completion for command names
and file paths. It supports POSIX-style quoting, output redirection, five
builtin commands, and external command execution via fork/execvp/waitpid.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-rushx\-shell
Start the interactive shell REPL instead of the GUI terminal emulator. This
flag is also used internally by the terminal emulator when it re-executes itself
to spawn the shell child process.
.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.B exit
Terminate the shell immediately.
.TP
.B echo \fR[\fB\-e\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fIARGS\fR...]
Print arguments to stdout, joined by spaces. \fB\-n\fR suppresses the trailing
newline. \fB\-e\fR enables interpretation of escape sequences: \\n (newline),
\\r (carriage return), \\t (tab), \\\\ (backslash), \\e (ESC), and \\0NNN
(octal byte). Flags can be combined (\fB\-en\fR, \fB\-ne\fR).
.TP
.B type \fICOMMAND\fR
Report whether COMMAND is a shell builtin or an external executable. For
externals, prints the resolved path. If not found, prints an error.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the current working directory.
.TP
.B cd \fR[\fIDIR\fR]
Change the working directory. Supports \fB~\fR (home), \fB~/subdir\fR
(home-relative), \fB\-\fR (previous directory), and absolute or relative paths.
.SH TAB COMPLETION
Pressing Tab on the first word completes command names by searching builtins and
executable files in PATH (filtered by execute permission). On subsequent words,
Tab completes file and directory names; directories are suffixed with /. A single
match auto-fills with a trailing space; multiple matches extend to their longest
common prefix; a second Tab lists all candidates. No match rings the bell.
.SH REDIRECTION
Output redirection is supported:
.PP
.RS
\fBcommand\fR > \fIfile\fR (stdout, truncate)
.br
\fBcommand\fR >> \fIfile\fR (stdout, append)
.br
\fBcommand\fR 2> \fIfile\fR (stderr, truncate)
.br
\fBcommand\fR 2>> \fIfile\fR (stderr, append)
.RE
.PP
Stdout and stderr can be redirected independently in the same command. Input
redirection (<) and pipes (|) are not yet implemented.
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve all characters literally. Double quotes allow backslash
escapes for \\, ", $, `, and newline. An unquoted backslash escapes the
immediately following character.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell returns 0 on normal exit via the \fBexit\fR builtin or EOF. External
commands that exit with a non-zero status have their exit code printed to stderr.
.SH EXAMPLES
Launch the GUI terminal:
.PP
.RS
.B rushx
.RE
.PP
Run the shell directly:
.PP
.RS
.B rushx \-\-rushx\-shell
.RE
.PP
Redirect output to a file:
.PP
.RS
.B echo hello > output.txt
.RE
.PP
Redirect stderr:
.PP
.RS
.B ls /nonexistent 2> errors.log
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
CSI sequences for cursor positioning (CUP, CUU, CUD, CUF, CUB) are discarded;
programs that rely on cursor addressing (vim, htop, less) will not render
correctly. There is no scrollback buffer, no pipeline support, no variable or
glob expansion, no signal handling beyond kernel defaults, and no runtime
configuration file.
